-- Q4515: Materiality [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Misstatements below overall materiality could still influence users'' economic decisions for those items","Every account balance must have its own separate materiality level by default","A lower threshold helps reduce the total audit fee charged to the client","Management specifically requests a lower materiality for certain sensitive accounts"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 320, specific materiality is set when the auditor determines that misstatements of lesser amounts than overall materiality could reasonably influence users'' decisions for particular classes of transactions, balances, or disclosures. Common examples include related party transactions and executive compensation.
Wrong (B): Specific materiality is not automatically required for every account. It applies only when the auditor identifies particular items warranting a lower threshold.
Wrong (C): Reducing audit fees is not a valid basis for establishing specific materiality. The determination is driven by user sensitivity, not cost considerations.
Wrong (D): Management does not set or request materiality thresholds. Materiality is solely the auditor''s professional judgment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4515;

-- Q4516: Materiality [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Evaluate whether the audit strategy and plan require revision as a result","Only revise materiality upward, never revise it downward","Notify the client before making any revision to materiality","Defer all materiality revisions until the final evaluation stage"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 320.13, when materiality is revised during the audit, the auditor must determine whether the overall audit strategy and audit plan need revision. A downward revision may require additional procedures to address the higher risk.
Wrong (B): Materiality can be revised either upward or downward based on changes in circumstances discovered during fieldwork.
Wrong (C): The auditor is not required to notify the client before revising materiality. It is the auditor''s independent professional judgment.
Wrong (D): Materiality should be reassessed whenever significant new information arises, not deferred until the final evaluation of misstatements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4516;

-- Q4521: Materiality [hard/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Set equal to the group materiality amount","Set higher than the group materiality amount","Set lower than the group materiality amount","Determined independently without reference to group materiality"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 600.21, component materiality must be set lower than group materiality for the financial statements as a whole. This ensures that the aggregate of uncorrected and undetected misstatements across all components does not exceed group materiality.
Wrong (A): Setting component materiality equal to group materiality would risk the aggregate of component misstatements exceeding the group threshold.
Wrong (B): A component materiality higher than group materiality would provide insufficient assurance at the group level.
Wrong (D): Component materiality must be established with explicit reference to group materiality to maintain consistency in the group audit.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4521;

-- Q4523: Materiality [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only the quantitative dollar amount of the misstatements","Only misstatements identified through substantive testing procedures","Both the size and nature of misstatements relative to affected accounts","Only individual misstatements that exceed performance materiality"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 450.11, the auditor must evaluate uncorrected misstatements by considering both quantitative (size) and qualitative (nature) factors in relation to particular classes of transactions, account balances, disclosures, and the financial statements as a whole.
Wrong (A): Limiting the evaluation to quantitative effects ignores qualitative factors such as the nature of misstatements or their effect on compliance with regulations.
Wrong (B): All identified misstatements must be evaluated, including those found through tests of controls, not just substantive testing.
Wrong (D): All accumulated misstatements are evaluated in the aggregate, not only those exceeding performance materiality.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4523;

-- Q4526: Responding to Assessed Risks [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Assigning more experienced staff and adding unpredictability to procedures","Reducing the number of audit staff assigned to the engagement","Lowering the materiality level to zero for complete coverage","Eliminating all tests of controls from the audit plan"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 330.05, overall responses to pervasive risks at the financial statement level include assigning more experienced staff, increasing supervision, and incorporating unpredictability. These address broad risks such as concerns about management integrity.
Wrong (B): Reducing staff would weaken the audit response. Higher risk calls for more resources and expertise, not fewer.
Wrong (C): Materiality cannot be set to zero. It is a judgment-based threshold, and zero would make the audit scope infinite and impractical.
Wrong (D): Eliminating tests of controls is not an appropriate response to heightened risk and would reduce the evidence base.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4526;

-- Q4527: Responding to Assessed Risks [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Are less extensive since the higher risk is already identified and documented","Are more persuasive through increased quantity or reliability of evidence","Focus exclusively on analytical procedures for the assertion","Rely primarily on inquiry of management about the assertion"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 330.07, when assessed risk is high the auditor designs procedures providing more persuasive evidence. This may mean larger sample sizes, more reliable sources (external vs. internal), or both to adequately respond to the risk.
Wrong (A): Higher assessed risk requires more extensive procedures, not less. Identifying the risk does not reduce the evidence needed.
Wrong (C): Analytical procedures alone are generally insufficient for high-risk assertions and should be supplemented with tests of details.
Wrong (D): Inquiry alone provides low-quality evidence and is inadequate as a sole response to a high assessed risk.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4527;

-- Q4528: Responding to Assessed Risks [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["When the audit procedures are performed during the engagement","How many items are selected and tested in the sample","The type and purpose of the procedures selected by the auditor","The total cost of performing the planned audit procedures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 330.A4, the nature of audit procedures refers to their type and purpose — inspection, observation, inquiry, confirmation, recalculation, reperformance, or analytical procedures. Nature is one of three dimensions the auditor adjusts in response to assessed risks.
Wrong (A): When procedures are performed describes the timing of audit procedures, not the nature.
Wrong (B): How many items are tested describes the extent of audit procedures, not the nature.
Wrong (D): Cost is a practical consideration but is not one of the three dimensions (nature, timing, extent) of audit procedures.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4528;

-- Q4529: Responding to Assessed Risks [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Treat it identically to any other assessed risk of misstatement","Rely solely on management inquiry about revenue recognition policies","Design procedures specifically responsive to the significant risk identified","Issue a qualified opinion due to the existence of a significant risk"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 330.21, the auditor must design and perform procedures specifically responsive to significant risks. These may include targeted substantive procedures, tests of controls, or a combination. Routine procedures alone are insufficient.
Wrong (A): Significant risks require special audit consideration beyond the standard response applied to other assessed risks.
Wrong (B): Inquiry alone is insufficient for any significant risk. Substantive procedures, including tests of details, are required.
Wrong (D): The existence of a significant risk does not require opinion modification. It requires a targeted audit response.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4529;

-- Q4530: Responding to Assessed Risks [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The budget allocated for the planned audit procedures","Whether procedures are performed at interim or at period end","The specific type of audit procedures the auditor selects","The number of items included in the auditor''s sample"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 330.A5, the timing of audit procedures refers to when they are performed — at an interim date or at period end. Testing at interim may be efficient but creates risk that misstatements in the remaining period go undetected.
Wrong (A): The budget is a practical constraint but does not define the timing dimension of audit procedures.
Wrong (C): The type of procedures selected describes the nature of audit procedures, not their timing.
Wrong (D): The number of items in the sample describes the extent of audit procedures, not their timing.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4530;

-- Q4531: Responding to Assessed Risks [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept management representations that override has not occurred","Test journal entries, review estimates for bias, and evaluate unusual transactions","Only test journal entries posted by the chief financial officer","Perform these procedures only when fraud is actually suspected"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 240.32 requires three mandatory procedures regardless of assessed risk: (1) testing the appropriateness of journal entries and adjustments, (2) reviewing accounting estimates for bias, and (3) evaluating the business rationale for significant unusual transactions.
Wrong (A): Management representations alone are insufficient to address the presumed risk of override. The auditor must perform specific procedures.
Wrong (C): Journal entry testing must cover entries from all personnel, not only those posted by the CFO.
Wrong (D): These procedures are mandatory on every engagement because management override is a presumed risk, not contingent on fraud suspicion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4531;

-- Q4532: Responding to Assessed Risks [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The quantity of a specific procedure, such as sample size selected","The variety of different audit procedures the team selects","When the procedures are performed during the audit engagement","The geographic locations where the audit procedures are performed"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 330.A6, the extent of audit procedures refers to how much of a specific procedure is performed — for example, the sample size for substantive testing or the number of control observations. Greater extent is appropriate when assessed risk is higher.
Wrong (B): The variety of different procedures relates to the nature of audit procedures, not the extent.
Wrong (C): When procedures are performed describes the timing dimension, not the extent.
Wrong (D): Geographic locations may affect planning logistics, but extent specifically refers to the quantity of a given procedure.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4532;

-- Q4533: Responding to Assessed Risks [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Reduce the number of inventory observations at year-end","Perform only inquiry and analytical procedures on valuation","Engage a specialist, test larger samples, and inspect additional locations","Rely on the prior year''s inventory valuation testing results"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 330, when inventory valuation risk is high, the auditor should respond with more robust procedures. This may include engaging a specialist for complex valuations, testing larger samples for proper pricing, and inspecting inventory at additional locations.
Wrong (A): Reducing observations would weaken the audit response when higher risk demands stronger evidence.
Wrong (B): Inquiry and analytical procedures alone are insufficient for a high-risk assertion. Tests of details are needed.
Wrong (D): Prior year results do not address current-year valuation risk and cannot substitute for current testing.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4533;

-- Q4535: Responding to Assessed Risks [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Reduces the overall cost of the audit engagement","Improves the efficiency of standard audit procedures","Deters fraud by making audit procedures less predictable to management","Satisfies specific regulatory reporting requirements for auditors"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 240.30, incorporating unpredictability helps detect fraud by preventing management and employees from anticipating specific procedures, timing, or items tested. Examples include surprise counts and testing entries from unusual periods.
Wrong (A): Unpredictability is not designed to reduce costs. It may actually increase costs by requiring procedures outside routine plans.
Wrong (B): Unpredictability is a fraud detection measure, not an efficiency technique. It may reduce efficiency in exchange for stronger fraud coverage.
Wrong (D): While auditing standards require considering unpredictability, it is not driven by regulatory reporting requirements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4535;

-- Q4536: Responding to Assessed Risks [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Is likely to result in a qualified audit opinion","Requires special audit consideration due to its nature or magnitude","Automatically requires the auditor to disclaim an opinion","Cannot be addressed by any available audit procedure"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 315.28, a significant risk is an identified risk of material misstatement that requires special audit consideration in the auditor''s professional judgment. Factors include complexity, subjectivity, susceptibility to fraud, and estimation uncertainty.
Wrong (A): A significant risk does not automatically lead to a qualified opinion. It requires a targeted audit response, not opinion modification.
Wrong (C): Disclaiming an opinion is reserved for scope limitations, not for the identification of significant risks.
Wrong (D): Significant risks are addressable through specifically designed procedures. Their identification triggers a targeted response, not an inability to audit.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4536;

-- Q4537: Responding to Assessed Risks [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Perform additional procedures to cover the remaining period to year-end","Issue the audit report as of the interim testing date instead","Accept management representations to cover the remaining period","Reduce the scope of year-end procedures given interim work performed"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 330.12, when substantive procedures are performed at an interim date, the auditor must perform additional substantive procedures, tests of controls, or a combination for the remaining period. This bridges the gap between interim testing and period end.
Wrong (B): The audit report must be dated no earlier than the date sufficient appropriate evidence has been obtained, not the interim date.
Wrong (C): Management representations cannot substitute for audit procedures needed to cover the remaining period.
Wrong (D): Testing at interim creates risk of undetected misstatements in the remaining period, requiring more procedures, not fewer.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4537;

-- Q4538: Responding to Assessed Risks [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only analytical procedures applied to revenue transactions","Only tests of controls over the revenue recognition process","Substantive procedures specifically designed to address the risk","Only inquiry of management about revenue recognition policies"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 330.21, for significant risks the auditor must perform substantive procedures specifically responsive to that risk. If the approach consists only of substantive procedures, those must include tests of details — analytical procedures alone are insufficient.
Wrong (A): Analytical procedures alone do not provide sufficiently precise evidence for significant risks and must include tests of details.
Wrong (B): Tests of controls alone do not satisfy the requirement for substantive procedures responsive to a significant risk.
Wrong (D): Inquiry alone is the weakest form of evidence and is insufficient as the sole response to any significant risk.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4538;

-- Q4540: Responding to Assessed Risks [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Have a clear linkage between assessed risks and the planned procedures","Be identical for all risks regardless of their assessed level","Focus only on assertions with the highest assessed risk level","Be limited exclusively to substantive analytical procedures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 330.06, further audit procedures must have a clear linkage between assessed risks at the assertion level and the nature, timing, and extent of procedures. This ensures the audit plan is responsive to specific identified risks.
Wrong (B): Procedures should be tailored to each risk''s assessed level. A uniform approach ignores differences in risk magnitude and nature.
Wrong (C): All relevant assertions require appropriate procedures, not only those assessed at the highest level.
Wrong (D): Substantive analytical procedures are one tool among many. The auditor selects from all available procedure types based on the assessed risk.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4540;

-- Q4542: Responding to Assessed Risks [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A decrease in the assessed risk of material misstatement","Management''s preference for less disruption during interim periods","A reduction in the overall audit budget for the engagement","An increase in assessed risk since the interim testing was performed"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 330.13, shifting from interim to period-end testing is typically driven by an increase in assessed risk. Testing at period end provides stronger evidence because it covers the full reporting period and eliminates remaining-period risk.
Wrong (A): A decrease in assessed risk would support keeping interim testing, not shifting to period end. Lower risk allows more flexibility in timing.
Wrong (B): Management''s scheduling preferences are not an appropriate basis for audit timing decisions, which must be risk-driven.
Wrong (C): Budget constraints are practical considerations but do not drive the professional judgment to shift testing to period end.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4542;

-- Q4544: Responding to Assessed Risks [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only substantive procedures with no reference to control effectiveness","Only management inquiry about whether controls are working","Only analytical procedures applied to the relevant assertions","Tests of controls to obtain evidence of operating effectiveness"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 330.08, when the risk assessment includes an expectation that controls are operating effectively, the auditor must test those controls to obtain evidence of operating effectiveness. The auditor cannot simply assume controls work without evidence.
Wrong (A): If the risk assessment relies on control effectiveness, the auditor must test those controls — not ignore them and use only substantive procedures.
Wrong (B): Inquiry alone is insufficient to obtain evidence about the operating effectiveness of controls.
Wrong (C): Analytical procedures do not test whether controls are operating effectively and would not validate the risk assessment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4544;

-- Q4545: Responding to Assessed Risks [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept management''s valuation without performing further testing","Rely on the prior year''s treatment of similar derivative instruments","Exclude the derivative transactions from the scope of the audit","Design specific substantive procedures to test valuation and classification"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 330, complex derivatives near year-end present significant valuation, classification, and disclosure risks. The auditor should design specific substantive procedures, which may include using a specialist, testing fair value inputs, and confirming terms with counterparties.
Wrong (A): Accepting management''s valuation without testing fails to obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence for a high-risk area.
Wrong (B): Prior year treatments do not address current-year transactions, which may have different terms, market conditions, or accounting requirements.
Wrong (C): Excluding material transactions from scope is not appropriate and could result in a scope limitation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4545;

-- Q4547: Using the Work of Others [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept the specialist''s findings without any further evaluation","Evaluate the specialist''s competence, objectivity, and work relevance","Require that the specialist be employed directly by the audit firm","Ignore the specialist''s work and perform fully independent procedures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 500.08, when management uses a specialist, the auditor must evaluate the specialist''s competence, capabilities, and objectivity. The auditor also assesses whether the findings are relevant and reasonable for audit purposes.
Wrong (A): The auditor cannot accept specialist findings uncritically. Evaluation of competence, objectivity, and reasonableness is required.
Wrong (C): Management''s specialist need not be employed by the audit firm. The distinction is between management''s specialist and the auditor''s specialist.
Wrong (D): Ignoring relevant specialist work is unnecessary. The auditor can use it as evidence after proper evaluation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4547;

-- Q4550: Using the Work of Others [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Ignore the service organization since it is not the audit client","Obtain an understanding of how those services affect internal control","Require the client to bring all transaction processing in-house","Issue a disclaimer because the service organization cannot be tested"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 402.09, the auditor must understand how the service organization''s services affect the client''s internal control relevant to the audit. The auditor should assess risks and determine whether sufficient evidence can be obtained through SOC reports or direct testing.
Wrong (A): A service organization that processes significant transactions is part of the client''s information system and cannot be ignored.
Wrong (C): The auditor has no authority to require the client to change its business operations or service arrangements.
Wrong (D): A disclaimer is not automatic. The auditor can obtain evidence through SOC reports, complementary user entity controls, or alternative procedures.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4550;

-- Q4551: Using the Work of Others [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only describes the design of the service organization''s controls","Includes both a description and tests of operating effectiveness over time","Is prepared by the user organization rather than the service auditor","Does not require involvement of an independent service auditor"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 402, a Type 2 SOC 1 report includes a description of the service organization''s system plus tests of the operating effectiveness of controls over a specified period (typically 6-12 months), providing more useful evidence than a Type 1 report.
Wrong (A): Describing only the design of controls is what a Type 1 report does. A Type 2 report goes further by including effectiveness testing.
Wrong (C): SOC 1 reports are prepared by the service auditor based on engagement with the service organization, not by the user organization.
Wrong (D): Both Type 1 and Type 2 SOC 1 reports require an independent service auditor to perform the examination.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4551;

-- Q4552: Using the Work of Others [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Replace all substantive testing the external auditor would perform","Use their work as evidence or have them provide direct assistance","Eliminate the need to understand the client''s internal control","Allow the internal auditors to sign the external audit report"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 610, the external auditor may use internal audit work in two ways: (1) using work already performed as audit evidence, or (2) having internal auditors provide direct assistance under the external auditor''s supervision. The external auditor retains sole responsibility for the opinion.
Wrong (A): Internal audit work cannot replace all substantive testing. The external auditor must still perform procedures on significant risks and judgmental areas.
Wrong (C): The external auditor must always obtain an understanding of internal control regardless of whether internal audit work is used.
Wrong (D): Only the external auditor may sign the audit report. Internal auditors have no authority to issue the external audit opinion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4552;

-- Q4553: Using the Work of Others [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Whether the function reports to governance and is free from operational duties","The compensation levels of individual internal audit staff members","Whether the internal auditors each hold a CPA license","The number of years the internal audit department has been operating"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 610.A8, objectivity is assessed by examining whether the internal audit function reports to those charged with governance and is free from operational responsibilities that could compromise unbiased assessments. Reporting to the audit committee is a strong indicator of objectivity.
Wrong (B): Compensation levels are an administrative matter and do not directly indicate whether the internal audit function can perform work objectively.
Wrong (C): CPA licensure relates to competence, not objectivity. Internal auditors may hold other relevant certifications such as CIA.
Wrong (D): The department''s age does not determine objectivity. A new department with proper governance reporting can be highly objective.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4553;

-- Q4554: Using the Work of Others [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Increase direct testing because reliance on internal audit is limited here","Accept the internal auditors'' conclusions without further testing","Reduce the scope of the external audit in those judgmental areas","Delegate the significant judgments entirely to the internal audit team"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 610.12, in areas involving significant judgments such as estimates or going concern, the external auditor should limit reliance on internal audit work and increase the extent of direct testing. Greater judgment requires greater external auditor involvement.
Wrong (B): Accepting conclusions without testing in high-judgment areas fails to meet the external auditor''s responsibility for the opinion.
Wrong (C): Areas with significant judgments require more external auditor work, not less. Reducing scope would be inappropriate.
Wrong (D): The external auditor cannot delegate significant professional judgments to internal auditors and retains full responsibility.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4554;

-- Q4555: Using the Work of Others [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Is engaged or employed by the auditor to assist in obtaining evidence","Is always required to be a licensed certified public accountant","Must be a full-time employee of the external audit firm","Cannot provide evidence related to accounting estimates or valuations"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 620.06, an auditor''s specialist is engaged or employed by the auditor to assist in obtaining sufficient appropriate audit evidence. This distinguishes them from management''s specialist, who is engaged by management to help prepare the financial statements.
Wrong (B): An auditor''s specialist possesses expertise outside accounting or auditing and need not be a CPA. Examples include engineers, actuaries, and geologists.
Wrong (C): The specialist may be either an external contractor engaged by the firm or an internal employee. Full-time employment is not required.
Wrong (D): Specialists frequently provide evidence about accounting estimates and valuations, such as fair value measurements and reserve calculations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4555;

-- Q4556: Using the Work of Others [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Perform only analytical procedures on the component''s financial information","Rely solely on the component auditor''s report without further review","Perform or have performed an audit using component materiality","Exclude the significant component from the group audit entirely"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 600.27, for a component that is individually financially significant to the group, the group engagement team or a component auditor must perform an audit of the component''s financial information using component materiality. This ensures sufficient evidence for the group opinion.
Wrong (A): Analytical procedures alone are insufficient for individually significant components. A full audit of the financial information is required.
Wrong (B): The group engagement team cannot simply accept a component auditor''s report without evaluating the work and communications.
Wrong (D): Excluding an individually significant component from the group audit would result in a scope limitation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4556;

-- Q4558: Using the Work of Others [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Ignore the exceptions since the service auditor has already evaluated them","Automatically disclaim an opinion on the client''s financial statements","Evaluate whether exceptions affect controls relevant to the client","Require the service organization to reissue the report without exceptions"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 402.16, when a SOC 1 Type 2 report contains exceptions, the user auditor evaluates whether those exceptions indicate control deficiencies affecting the user entity''s financial statements. The auditor considers whether complementary controls mitigate the impact and whether additional procedures are needed.
Wrong (A): The user auditor cannot ignore exceptions. They must independently assess the impact on the user entity''s controls and financial statements.
Wrong (B): Exceptions in a SOC report do not automatically require a disclaimer. The impact must be evaluated before any opinion modification.
Wrong (D): The user auditor has no authority to require the service organization to reissue its SOC report.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4558;

-- Q4559: Using the Work of Others [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Review communications and findings to evaluate evidence sufficiency","Simply accept each component auditor''s report without any review","Visit every component and personally supervise all audit testing","Only review the component auditors'' billing statements for fees"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 600.42, the group engagement partner must review component auditor communications, evaluate significant findings and risks, and determine whether sufficient appropriate evidence has been obtained to support the group audit opinion.
Wrong (B): The group engagement partner cannot passively accept component reports. Active evaluation of communications and findings is required.
Wrong (C): Personal visits to every component are not required. The partner evaluates evidence through communications, reports, and professional judgment.
Wrong (D): Billing statements are administrative documents that provide no information about the sufficiency or appropriateness of audit evidence.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4559;
